---
import { getCollection, render } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const raw = (await getCollection('research')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
const items = await Promise.all(
	raw.map(async (r) => ({ data: r.data, Content: (await render(r)).Content }))
);
---

<Base title="Research" description="Building a faithful in-silico reconstruction of the whole brain, to understand cognition in neurobiologically meaningful, mechanistic terms.">
	<h1>Research</h1>

	<div class="arc">
		<p>
			The core principle of my scientific practice is best encapsulated by Feynman's famous quote,
			<em>“What I cannot create, I do not understand.”</em> This is why I study the brain with in-silico models.
			With them I can simulate the brain, then cut some connections and poke some brain regions, none of
			which is possible in the human brain. Once I obtain a circuit model of the brain with sufficient
			alignment to the biological data, I can also make it a recurrent neural network (RNN) that solves
			tasks, to see how the brain actually computes.
		</p>
		<p>
			My aim goes beyond understanding the brain. We should then be able to know how to fix it when it
			goes wrong. Psychopathology is a major societal problem and is becoming more so. Once we have a
			faithful in-silico brain model, we should be able to understand the pathologies in more precise
			terms: what circuits, dynamical properties, and regions underlie schizophrenia, bipolar disorder,
			and autism.
		</p>
		<p>
			This is a long journey, and its first chapter is already written. The hierarchy that I retrieved
			from fMRI in the human brain ordered brain regions from the sensory and motor regions (the most
			external) to the paralimbic regions (the most internal), which sit right above the limbic system
			and the subcortex. This implies that the most important signals come from the regions that process
			internal, bodily information. It is empirical evidence for a theoretical perspective (which I
			co-led in an incredible team), grounded in cybernetics, on how to build artificial intelligence in
			a life-like way, so that it behaves like us, with shared directions. There we proposed that internal
			signals form the universal context for cognition (more below).
		</p>
	</div>

	{
		items.map(({ data, Content }) => (
			<article class="entry research-entry">
				<h2 class="research-title">{data.title}</h2>
				<div class="research-body">
					<Content />
				</div>
				{data.status && <p class="entry-meta">{data.status}</p>}
				{data.links.length > 0 && (
					<p class="links-inline">
						{data.links.map((l) => (
							<a href={l.href} target={l.href.startsWith('http') ? '_blank' : undefined} rel="noopener">
								{l.label}
							</a>
						))}
					</p>
				)}
			</article>
		))
	}
</Base>

<style>
	.arc { margin-bottom: 2.4rem; }
	.research-entry { margin-bottom: 2.6rem; }
	.research-title { font-size: 1.2rem; margin-top: 2rem; }
	.research-body :global(p) { margin: 0.4rem 0 0.5rem; }
</style>
